Bridly increasing the cumulative incidence of Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) within the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom between 1 January and 15 March 2020
Cumulative incidence of Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) shows a similar trend in EU/European Economic Area countries and the United Kingdom, thus confirming that, despite being at different stages, the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly in all countries.
Based on Italy’s experience, countries, hospitals, and intensive care services need to prepare for a spike in patients with COVID-19 in need of care, in particular intensive care.
On December 31, 2019, an outbreak of unidentified etiological pneumonia was reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On January 9, 2020, the Chinese Center for Disease Prevention and Control designated the responsible agent as a new coronavirus now known as Coronavirus 2, Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease resulting from SARS-CoV-2 infection has been dubbed Coronavirus Disease (COVID-19).
To date, about 80 per cent of people with COVID-19 have a benign condition, i.e. an infection of the airways with or without pneumonia, most of which are cured.
In about 14% of the cases, COVID-19 results in more severe damage requiring hospitalization, while the remaining 6% develop a serious form of disease requiring intensive care.
Mortgage of patients hospitalised due to COVID-19 is around 4%.
As part of this study, we are evaluating the evolution of the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in each of the countries of the European Union/European Economic Area (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UK), and compare it with the developments in Hubei Province in China.
We are also comparing the current number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom with that of Italy for the period from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
Cases of COVID-19 in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom
After China, COVID-19 has continued to propagate, and the dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic in the rest of the world are currently following that of that country.
On March 11, 2020, the Managing Director of the World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID-19 a pandemic.
In the March 5 edition of Euromonitoring 2020, Spiteri et al. reported the first cases of COVID-19 confirmed in Europe according to the WHO case-definition.
Within the EU/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on 24 January 2020 among people returning from Wuhan in Hubei province, China.
As of 15 March 2020, cases of COVID-19 had been detected in each of the 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (U.I.R.) with 39,768 cases and 1,727 reported deaths between 31 December 2019 and 1,727, including 11,750 cases and 1,441 deaths for Italy alone.
Cumulative number and cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases
At the European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (CEPCM), the number of cases of COVID-19 reported in each country around the world obtained only from official sources such as ministries of health, national and regional health authorities in different countries as well as the WHO, is updated daily at 08.00 a.m.
These data have been used to assess the evolution of COVID-19 within the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, and to make a comparison with Italy’s experience.
In order to assess the pre-prevalence of active cases of COVID-19, we calculated the cumulative 14-day incidence of COVID-19 cases, thus taking into account the natural trends of COVID-19, within each EU/EEA country and the United Kingdom for the period from 1 January to 15 March 2020.
We have also compared the cumulative number of cases reported in each country at 08.00 a.m. at 8 a.m. with data from Italy for the period from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
Evolving COVID-19 in EU/EEA Countries and the United Kingdom
The change in the 14-day cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom has generally followed that of Hubei Province (China) (Figure 1).
For the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom, the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 began to grow around 21 February before experiencing a drastic increase around 28 February 2020
This development was mainly due to the rapid increase in the number of cases reported in Italy, but all the other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom presented a similar increase in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 (additional data).
Figure 2 presents the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom compared to Italy data for the period from 31 January to 15 March 2020.
It can be seen that on 15 March at 08 a.m., 15 other EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom had already reported a total number of cases comparable to that of Italy only 3 weeks earlier or fewer.
Our results indicate that the number of reported cases of COVID-19 is increasing rapidly within the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Changing the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 suggests that the pandemic is progressing at a comparable speed in all countries.
This is despite the fact that countries are at different stages and there are differences in the responses of national public health services, potential variations in case definitions as well as differences in patient selection regimens to be tested for confirmation of COVID-19, including for catch-remotive testing.
In early March 2020, physicians in affected regions in Italy reported that about 10% of COVID-19 patients needed intensive care, and the media reported that hospitals and intensive care services in these regions had already reached their maximum capacity.
Data on the admission of COVID-19 cases in hospitals and/or intensive care services are currently available at EU/EEA level for only 6% and 1% of cases, respectively (data not presented).
It is, however, necessary to carry out a systematic collection in order to supplement the current surveillance data that focus on the number of reported cases and deaths.
A study carried out in 2010–11 showed a significant discrepancy in the number of beds available in intermediate and intensive care services in Europe, ranging from 29.2 beds per 100 000 individuals in Germany to 4x2 beds in Portugal.
This means that more or less countries have more or less resources than Italy (12.5 intensive and intermediate bedrops per 100 000 in 2010–11).
Modelling scenarios related to the saturation of care capacity, with estimates for each EU/EEA country and the United Kingdom on the prevalence of in-hospital COVID-19 cases associated with a hazard; 90 per cent exceedance of intensive care bed capacity are provided in the sixth update of the ECDC COVID-19 Rapid Risk Assessment.
Given that the cases were previously grouped together in parts of the EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom, hospitals and intensive care services generally receive a defined regional population, data on cases and beds in intensive care should preferably be established at Level 2 of the Classification of Territorial Territorial Unit Statistical Units (NUTS 2).
Italy’s experience and current developments in other countries show that the COVID-19 pandemic is progressing rapidly within the EU/EEA and the United Kingdom.
Countries, hospitals and intensive care services therefore need to be prepared for a scenario of sustained community transmission of SARS-CoV-2 as well as an increase in the number of patients suffering from COVID-19 requiring care, in particular intensive care, such as the affected regions of Italy.
As pointed out in the recent Rapid Risk Assessment of ECDC, a rapid, proactive and comprehensive approach is essential to delay the spread of SARS-COV-2, moving from a containment approach to an attenuation approach, as the rapid and early increase in the number of cases may not give decision-makers and hospitals sufficient time to understand, accept and adapt their response accordingly, if nothing had been implemented upstream.
Prompt risk assessment also identifies public health measures to mitigate the impact of the pandemic.
There is a brief window during which each country will be able to step up its monitoring efforts in order to slow down the spread of SARS-CoV-2 and reduce the pressure on health services.
Otherwise, health-care systems in other EU/EEA countries are likely to face a spike of patients requiring intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
Corporatia’s coronavirus outbreak 2019 (COVID-19), caused by Coronavirus 2, Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) (SARS-CoV-2), has so far resulted in more than 3,000 deaths and infected more than 80,000 people in China and around the world, causing a humanitarian disaster.
Like its counterpart, SARS-CoV, which led to thousands of SARS cases in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 could be transmitted by bats and causes similar symptoms through a similar mechanism.
However, COVID-19 exhibits less severity and mortality than SARS, but it is much more transmissive, and it affects older people more than young people and more men than women.
In response to the rapid increase in the number of publications on this emerging disease, this article attempts to provide a timely and comprehensive review of this burgeoning research topic.
We will address the fundamentals of epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and disease prevention.
Although many of the questions are still awaiting answers, we hope that this review will contribute to a better understanding and eradication of this serious disease.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020, has forever marked the Chinese, who have been forced to vacate for the entire duration of the golden week as well as for long other weeks due to the spread of a new viral disease.
Coronavioau (CoV) is highly homologous to the Coronatric Coronatric (CoV), causing an outbreak of Severe Acute Respiratory Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) in 2003 and SARS-CoV-2 by the World Health Organization (WHO) on 11 February 2020, and the Coronavirus-Cravirus 19 (COVID-19).
The epidemic began in Wuhan and quickly spread throughout China before extending to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of 2 March 2020, the virus involved more than 80,000 confirmed cases of COVID-19, with more than 4 40,000 patients being hospitalized and more than 3,000 deaths.
For the WHO, COVID-19 is “Public Enemy Number One,” potentially more dangerous than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih/pubmed/), in less than two months, more than 200 studies have been published on COVID-19, notably in the fields of virology, epidemiology, aetiology, diagnosis and treatment, since the first report on 7 January 2020 showing the virus sequence isolated from several patients.
This examination attempts to synthesize the progress of research on this burgeoning topic.
As soon as possible, we will try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and another Corporacial Coronavirus, Middle East Respiratory Physoma (MERS, an outbreak that took place in 2012).
We will also take stock of what we have learned so far in terms of the prevention and prognosis of the disease, and will address some issues that are still pending yet urgent.
Coronaviruses are traditionally regarded as non-lethal hoses for humans, causing mainly about 15% of common colds.
However, over the course of this century, we have experienced two highly man-domestroicious Coronates, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, resulting in an epidemic that started in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012, respectively, before spreading rapidly to many other countries with high morbidity and mortality.
The current COVID-19 is the third-largest coronavirus outbreak in recent history.
As presented in FIG. FIG. 1,1, outbreaks of pneumonia then of unknown origin were first reported in Wuhan on 31 December 2019 to the Chinese National Health Commission.
Seven days later, the Coronavirus sequence was unveiled.
On January 15, 2020, the first fatal case was reported in Wuhan.
In the meantime, the epidemic had spread rapidly to neighbouring cities, provinces, and countries.
On 20 January it was reported that healthcare professionals had been infected, suggesting that human transmission was possible.
On 23 January, the city of Wuhan quarangled all of its public transport.
On 24 January, the first clinical disease study indicated that, out of 41 confirmed cases, only 21 patients had been in direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market as the starting point for infection with an unfamiliar animal source.
On 30 January, the WHO described the global health-emergency epidemic.
At the time of the drafting of this report, the disease had already spread throughout China as well as to almost 50 other countries around the world (Fig. (Fig. 2).
As the situation evolves rapidly, the final scope and severity of the epidemic remain to be determined.
On February 11, 2020, a multi-centre study of 8,866 patients, including 4,021 confirmed cases of COVID-19, presented an up-to-date overview of the epidemic as follows (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX-rHPXa1qHA2 bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 inclemented people of all ages, but more predominantly in the 30-65 age group.
Almost half (47.7 per cent) of those infected were over the age of 50, very few were under the age of 20, and only 14 were under the age of 10.
SARS-CoV-2 inaccurated men (0.0/10100,000) rather than women (0.27/100,000).
COVID–19 was growing in homes, mainly in Hubei province and on the periphery.
The average duration between the onset of symptoms and the diagnosis of COVID-19 was 5 days (2-9).
The mean incubation period was 4.8 days (3.0-7.2).
The mean duration between onset of symptoms and death was 9.5 days (4.8-13).
The basic reproduction rate (R0) was 3.77 (95% CI: 3,51-4.05) and the Adadjusted R0 was 2.23-4.82.
Expectationed people had increased exponentially until January 23, 2020, coinciding with the massive movements that took place prior to the Spring Festival in China.
Mortality of patients identified as confirmed cases was 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%), and adjusted mortality for all patients was 3.06% (95 per cent CI: 2.02-4.59).
The three main risk factors for COVID-19 were sex (male), age 60 and severe pneumonia.
Coronavirus is a subfamilion of large enveloped viruses containing single-stranded RNA.
They can be categorized into four genus, namely alpha, beta, gamma and delta, the coronaviruses known to infect humans belonging to the alpha and beta genera.
Envelope spike glycoprotein (S) binds an angiotensification enzyme 2 (ACE2) and dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP4) cellular receptories for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, respectively, followed by membrane membranar fusion.
The viral RNA genome is released into the cytoplasm; after replication of the viral genome, genomic RNA accompanied by envelope glycoproteins and nucleocapside proteins forms vesicles containing virions, then fusing with the plasma membrane to release the virus.
The first genomic sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was presented on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 has been established as a new type of betacoronavirus, with more than 99.98 per cent genetic Identity among 10 sequequentated samples taken from the origin of the outbreak, the Huanan Sea Fruit Market in Wuhan.
SARS-CoV-2 is genetically closer to SARS-CoV than MERS-CoV.
Using transmission electron microscopy, SARS-CoV-2 particles were detected in ultra-thorine sections of human epithelium from the respiratory tract.
Human ACE2 has been identified as a SARS-CoV-2 receptor and SARS-CoV receptor.
However, SARS-CoV-2 S protein binds to human ACE2 less strongly than SARS-CoV, coinciding that SARS-CoV-2 causes less infection than SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form a new short protein encoded by orf3b as well as an obf8-encoded secreted protein.
Orf3b of SARS-CoV-2 could play a role in viral pathogenicity and inhibit IFN expression; however, the orf8 does not contain known domains or functional patterns.
On February 18, 2020, zhou et al., presented the cryo-ME structure of human ACE2-complete at a complex resolution of 2.9 degrees with amino acid transporter B0AT1.
They discovered that the complex, which exhibited open and closed conformations, was grouped as a dimer and that the ACE2-B0AT1 complex could bind two S proteins, which provides indications for coronavirus recognition and infection.
B0AT1 could become a therapeutic target for the screening of medicinal products in order to eliminate SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Original Host and Intermediate Spouse
It has been established that SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV originated from bats and were transmitted to humans via civets and cameliders, respectively.
Following a phylogenetic Comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with Other Coronavirus Coronavirus, bats have been advanced as the native host of SARS-CoV-2, since the new virus is 96% identical to the two SARS-type coronaviruses from bat-SL-CoV-X45 and bat-SL-CoV-X21.
However, the intermediate host that allowed the virus to cross the species barrier to infect humans remains unknown, and the route of transmission is still elucidate.
Ji, et al. hypothetized according to which the virus could have been transmitted from bats to humans by snakes, imply homologous recombination within the S-protein.
According to a study carried out by researchers in Guangzhou in China, Pangolin – a tweeted mammal feeding on ants, often used in traditional Chinese medicine – could be the intermediate host of SARS-CoV-2, judging by 99% genetic homology between a coronavirus discovered in pangolins and SARS-CoV-2.
However, a gap of 1 % between two genomes constitutes an important difference; it should therefore be looked forward to conclusive results making it possible to establish concrete evidence (Fig. (FIG. 33).
The physico-chemical properties of SARS-CoV-2 are still very little known.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive in vitro for 48 hours in a sequential environment for up to 5 days at a temperature of 20oC and humidity of 40 to 50 per cent.
SARS-CoV-2 could very well have similar properties.
SARS-CoV-2 appears to be sensitive to ultraviolet rays as well as at a temperature of 56oC for 30 minutes; ether, ethanol at 75 %, chlorine-based disinfectants, peracetic acid, chloroform and other greasy solvents, but not chlorhexidine can effectively inactivate the virus.
The human population as a whole is, in a general way, devoid of immunity to SARS-CoV-2 and is therefore vulnerable to the new virus.
At the present time, no detailed studies have been reported concerning the immunological response to SARS-CoV-2.
As a result, we can only refer only to studies previously carried out on other coronaviruses, notably SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Fig.
Usually when a virus infects a drowser, it is first recognised by its innate immune system through Molecular Pattern Recognition Receivers (PRRs), including C lectin receptors, Toll (TLR), NOD (NLR) receptors and RIG-I (RLR).
Alternatively, the virus induces the expression of flammatory factors, the maturation of dendritic cells and the synthesis of type I interferons (FINs) which limit the spread of the virus and accelerate the macrophocytosis phagocytosis of viral antigens.
However, SARS-CoV N protein can allow the virus to elude immune responses.
Soon, the adaptive immune response joins the fight against the virus.
The T lymphocytes, and in particular the T cells CD4' and CD8, play an important role in the defence.
CD4” T cells stimulate B cells that produce virus-generated antibodies, and CD8- T cells directly remove the infected cells.
Aid T cells produce pro-inflammatory cytokines to support defence cells.
However, Coronavirus can inhibit T-cell functions by inducing apoptosis.
Humoral Immunity with Supplements such as C3a and C5a and Antibodies is also essential to combat the viral infection.
For example, antibodies isolated from a recovered patient neutralised MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, an overreaction of the immune system causes a localised explosion in the number of free radicals, which can lead to serious damage to the lungs as well as to other organs and, in the worst-case scenario, multi-visceral failure or even death.
SHR-CoV-2 infection, characterised by occurrence in households, is more likely to affect the elderly with comorbidities and pregnant women.
It is commonly accepted that people exposed to a large number of viruses or whose immune functions are compromised are at greater risk of being infected than others.
It is estimated that the average incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 is 1 to 14 days, but more generally 3 to 7 days according to a study carried out of the first 425 cases reported in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases showed that the incubation period averaged 3 days, for a range from 0 to 24 days.
A more recent study, as described above, concluded an incubation period of 4.8 days (3.0-7.2) based on demography of 8,866 cases.
It is very important for the health authorities to adjust the actual quarantine period according to the most precise incubation period possible, in order to prevent infected but asymptomatic people from transmitting the virus.
Typically, people who are exposed to or are infected with the virus are quarantined for 14 days.
Should quarantine be extended to 24 days?
Fever is often the first and main symptom of COVID-19, and may or may not be accompanied by other symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headache, sore throat, rhinorrhoea, chest pain, diarrhoea, nause nausea and vovoking.
Some patients experienced dysp dyspno dupnoea and/or hypoxemia one week after the onset of the disease.
With regard to severe cases, patients quickly developed acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic ketoacidosis and coagulopathy.
Patients with fever and/or respiratory symptoms and those with acute fever, even in the absence of thoracic X-ray abnormalities, should be tested for the virus for early diagnosis.
A demographic study carried out at the end of December 2019 showed 98% fever, 76% dry cough, dysp dyspnoea at 55% and 3% diarrhea; 8% required ventilatory assistance.
Similar findings have been reported by two recent studies on a family and an outbreak that has occurred as a result of virus transmission from an asymptomatic individual.
In the same way, a population study carried out in 2012 showed that the main symptoms of patients with MERS-CoV were fever (98%), dry cough (47%) and dyspnoea (55%).
However, 80% required ventilatory assistance, much more than patients with COVID-19, which is consistent with higher lethality of MERS compared to COVID-19.
Diarrhoeonia (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also seen in MERS patients.
Regarding SARS, it was established that the main symptoms were fever (99%-100 %), dry cough (29%-75%), dyspnoea (40%-42 %), diarrhoea (20%-25%) and sore throat (13%-25%), and ventilatory assistance was needed for approximately 14-20% of patients.
As at 14 February, COVID-19 mortality was 2 per cent compared to 66,576 cases confirmed worldwide.
Incomparably, SARS mortality in November 2002 was 10 per cent for 5,096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, according to a demographic study carried out in June 2012, mortality was 37% for 2,494 confirmed cases.
An earlier study reported that SARS-CoV-2 R0 reached 6.47 with a 95 per cent (CI) confidence interval of 5.71-7.23, while SARS-CoV R0 ranged from only 2 to 4.
Symptom-wrongment comparison of SARS-CoV2, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV is presented in Table1.1.
Previous data suggest that SARS-CoV-2 has greater ability to propagate than MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but is less lethal.
As a result, it is much more difficult to control the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak than has occurred for the MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV epidemics.
The appearance of a household often takes place within a family or within a rally or vehicle, as in the case of a cruise.
Patients have often travelled or resided in Wuhan or other affected areas, or have been in contact with infected individuals or patients within two weeks prior to symptoms.
However, it has been demonstrated that individuals may be carriers of the virus without symptoms for more than two weeks and that recovered patients may again carry the virus, which underlines the need to increase quarantine duration.
Patients experience a normal or reduced number of peripheral white blood cells (including lymphocyte cells) in the early stages.
e.g. lymphopenia with white-collgor blood cell counts such as 4 x 109/L and number of zlt lymphocytes; 1 x 109/L, as well as high aspartate aminotransferase and viremia were identified in 1,099 patients suffering from COVID-19.
Some patients had high levels of loroceties, muscle enzymes and myoglobin in the blood, and most had an increase in C-recustivity protein and erthrocyte sedimentation.
In the most serious cases, the level of D-dimer, a blembrobeth degradation product in the blood, was high, and the lymphocyte count gradually decreased.
Thoracic X-ray abnormalities occur in most patients with COVID-19, characterised by unequal bilateral shades or defrosted glass opacity in the lungs.
Patients often develop an atypical pneumonia, an acute lung lesion and an Acute Respiratory Respiratory Diffusing Syndrome (ASD).
Under SDRA, uncontrolled ignition, fluid build-up and progressive fibrosis severely compromise gas exchanges.
Difficultation of pneumocyte type I and type II results a decrease in surfactant levels and an increase in surface tension, thus reducing the capacity of the lungs to swell and increasing the risk of pulmonary collapse.
As a result, the most worrisome thoracic examinations often coincide with the most severe form of the disease.
On 18 February 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 revealed pneumocyte desquamation, hyaline mummine formation, interstitial lymphocyte infiltration and the presence of multi-nucleated syncytial cells in the lungs of a patient who has died of the disease, which coincides with the pathology of viral infection and DRSA and is similar to that of patients suffering from SARS.
Detection of SARS-CoV-2 RNA via Reintermised Transcription Polymerase Chain Reaction (RT-PCR) was used as the primary criterion for the diagnosis of COVID-19.
However, due to the high rate of falsaries, which can accelerate the epidemic, the clinical manifestations began to be used for diagnosis (then no longer relied solely on RT-PCR) in China on 13 February 2020.
A similar situation had been observed for the diagnosis of SARS.
For example, the combination of medical histories, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests and radiological examinations is essential and imperative for an effective diagnose.
On February 14, 2020, the Feng zhang group described a SHERLOCK protocol based on CRISPR technology for SARS-CoV-2 detection purposes, which makes it possible to identify synthetic fragments of SARS-CoV-2 RNA between 20 x 10-18 mol/L and 200 x 10-18 mol/L (10-100 copies per microliter of sample) without reactivates.
It is to be hoped that this new technique will significantly improve the sensitivity and practicality if its use on clinical specimens is conclusive.
Due to a lack of experience vis-à-vis the novel Coronavirus, physicians can only offer supportive care to patients with COVID-19, while testing a variety of therapies that have already been used or offered for the treatment of other coronaviruses, such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, as well as other viral diseases (Table (Table 2).
These therapies include existing and potential therapies with antiviral drugs, immunosuppressants, steroids, re-patient plasma, Chinese medicine as well as psychological support.
Same plasma from recreated patients has been proposed for treatment.
Pharmaceutical companies are all working to develop antibodies and vaccines against the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 mainly attacks the lungs in the first place and probably also, to a lesser extent, to other organs expressing ACE2, such as the gastrointestinal system and the kidneys.
Nevertheless, dysfunction and respiratory insufficiency constitute the main threat to patients and the leading cause of death.
For example, respiratory assistance is essential for relieving symptoms and saving lives; this includes general oxygen therapy, high-output oxygen therapy, non-invasive ventilation and invasive mechanical ventilation, depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients suffering from severe respiratory symptoms should undergo extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (OMEC), a modified cardiopulmonary derivation technique used for the treatment of critical cardiac or respiratory deficiencies.
In addition, the maintenance of electrolyte equilibrium, the prevention and treatment of secondary infections and septic shocks, as well as the protection of vital organ functions, are also essential for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
It has been established that an excessive reaction of the immune system causes cytokinetic shock in patients with SARS and MERS.
Cytokinetic shock is a form of systemic inflammatory response characterized by the release of an entire series of cytokines, in particular TNF-, IL-1, IL-2, IL-6, IFN, IFN, IFN' and MCP-1.
These cytokines induce immune cells to unleash a large number of free radicals, which are the main cause of ADR SDS and multi-visceral failure.
Immunosuppression is essential in the treatment of cytokinetic shocks, especially in severe cases.
Corticosteroids and tocilizumab an anti-IL6 monoclonal antibody were used to treat cytokinetic shocks.
Other anti-opposopled therapies for cytokine shocks include Immune Response Modulation by T-Chicle, Blocking IFN, IL-1 and TNF, JAK Inhibition, blinatumomab, cytokine reporting suppressor and HDAC inhibitors.
Steriloids as immunosuppressive agents were widely used in the treatment of SARS to reduce the severity of inflammatory lesions.
However, high-dose steriloid intake had no beneficial effect on serious lung lesions in patients suffering from SARS and COVID-19.
Instead, they would lead to serious side-effects, such as an avascular osteonecrosis, thus significantly affecting the prognosis.
However, the cautious use of short cycles of low to moderate dose corticosteroids has been recommended for patients with a serious form of COVID-19.
At the time of the drafting of this report, no effective antiviral therapy has yet been confirmed.
However, the intravenous administration of remdesivir, a nucleoside analog, has been shown to be effective in an American patient suffering from COVID-19.
Remdesivir is a new antiviral medicine originally developed by Gilead for the treatment of diseases caused by the Ebola and Marburg viruses.
Subsequently, remdesivir also demonstrated a possible inhibition of other single-strand-strength viruses, including MERS and SARS.
Based on these findings, Gilead transmitted the compound to China to allow for a number of trials on individuals infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results are highly awaited.
In addition, Baricitinib, Interferon, Lopsecuting/ritonavir and ribavirin were advanced as potential treatments for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhoeonia, nausea, vovomit, liver injury and other adverse reactions may occur as a result of combined treatment with lopinavir/ritonavir.
The interaction of these treatments with other medicines used in patients should be carefully monitored.
Plasma of recovered patients and production of antibodies
Gamming blood in patients who have cursed a contagious disease in order to treat other patients suffering from the same disease or to protect healthy individuals from this disease is an all-contact practice.
This is because recovered patients often have a relatively high level of antibodies against the pathogen.
Antibodies are an Immunostoimmune (Ig) produced by B-lymphocyte to combat pathogens and other foreign bodies; they recognise unique molecules in pathogens and neutralise them directly.
Plasma was extracted from the blood of a group of patients cured from COVID-19 and injected 10 seriously affected patients.
Symptoms improved within 24 hours, in combination with a decrease in inflammation, a reduction in viral load and an increase in oxygen saturation in the blood.
However, verification and clarification are necessary in order to be able to propose this method for large-scale use as long as no specific treatment has been developed.
Furthermore, given the therapeutic effects, certain drawbacks associated with plasma should be carefully discussed.
e.g., antibodies can lead to excessive stimulation of the immune response and cause cytokine-releasing syndrome, potentially fatal.
Antiboggle concentration in the blood is generally low, and the demand for plasma to treat seriously ill patients is significant.
It is difficult to develop and produce specific antibodies quickly enough to combat a global epidemic.
As a result, Iolating B-cells of re-recorded patients is more important and effective and more effective in identifying genetic codes encoding the relevant antibodies or looking for antibodies that are effective against the virus’s main proteins.
This would make it possible to rapidly increase the production of antibodies.
MTC has been used for millennia in China to treat a wide variety of diseases.
However, its effects rely largely on the combination of multiple components into a formula that varies according to the diagnose, based on TCM theories.
Most efficient components remain little or not known at all insofar as it is difficult to extract and verify them, or to know their optimal associations.
At the present time, due to the lack of a specific effective COVID-19 treatment, TCM is one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or remission of a serious form of the disease.
e.g. Shu Feng Jie Du and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules have been shown to be effective for the treatment of COVID-19.
The best COVID-19 cure rates were observed in TCM provinces of 8.7% of their patients, notably Gansu Province (63.7%), Ningxia region (50%) and Hunan (50%), while Hubei Province, which used only 30% of COVID-19 patients, had the lowest recovery rate (13%).
However, this is a rather approximate comparison since many other impact factors, such as the number of patients and the severity of the disease, should be taken into account for the evaluation.
On February 18, 2020, he and his collaborators published a study that compared treatment using Western Medicine (MOs) only with a combination of MO and MTC.
It transpired that the delay in returning to a normal body temperature, the delay in disappearance of the symptoms and the duration of wading was markedly shorter for the MO-MTC group than for the MO group only.
More importantly, the rate of worsening of symptoms (from mild to severe) was significantly lower for the MO-MTC group than for the MO group only (7.4% versus 46.2%); similarly, mortality was lower for the MO-MTC group than for the MO-MTC group only (8.8% versus 39%).
Nevertheless, the efficacy and safety of TCM have yet to be clarified through controlled trials on a larger scale and in more centres.
It would also be interesting to characterise the mechanism of action and clarify the effective components of TCM treatments or their associations, if possible.
Individuals identified as likely or confirmed cases of COVID-19 generally feel great concern about this highly contagious and lethal disease, and quarantined individuals also feel boredom, loneliness and erratic.
In addition, symptoms of the infection, such as fever, hypoxia and cavity, as well as the side effects of treatments, such as corticosteroid-induced insomnia, can lead to increased anxiety and psychological distress.
In the early stages of the SARS epidemic, multiple psychiatric burial disorders were reported, including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor excitement, psychotic symptoms, an acute confounding state and even suicidal tendencies.
Compulsory contact tracing and quarantine, as part of the public health services’ response to the COVID-19 epidemic, can increase the anxiety and guilt of the patients vis-à-vis the effects of conflication, quarantine and the stigmatization of their families and friends.
For example, mental health care must be provided to patients with COVID-19, individuals identified as probable cases, people in contact with these populations, as well as any others in distress.
Such psychological support must include the training of multidisciplinary teams, clear communication with regular and accurate updates on the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic and treatment plans, as well as the use of professional applications and electronic equipment in order to avoid close contact.
Succeeding vaccines are essential for interrupting the chain of transmission of infected animal reservoirs and humans to vulnerable hosts, and are often complementary to the antiviral treatment in controlling epidemics caused by emerging viruses.
Efforts have been made to develop S-protein-based vaccines to produce long-degrester antibodies in the long term and/or to induce protective immunity against SARS-CoV.
Livial attenuated vaccines have been evaluated in animal models for SARS.
However, the in- vivo efficacy of these potential vaccines in the elderly and on lethal-provised models as well as their protection against infection with a zoonotic virus has yet to be determined, since no clinical study has yet been initiated.
This is probably due to the fact that SARS went extinct 17 years ago and that no new cases have been reported since.
On the other hand, sporadic cases and foci of MERS continue to occur in the Middle East and in other regions owing to the persistence of zoonotic sources in enumerated areas.
Various vaccination strategies were developed for MERS using indebvacate virus, DNA plasmids, viral vectors, nanoparticles, virus-related particles and recombinant protein sub-units, and some were evaluated on animal models.
The development of a Safe and Effective SARS-CoV-2 Vaccine for non-immunised individuals is an urgent and key objective in order to control the ongoing epidemic.
However, this is a real challenge due to the significant delay (18 months on average) necessary to develop a vaccine and the dynamic variations of the coronavirus.
As an emergent disease, COVID-19 is just undergoing its clinical course across thousands of patients.
In most of the cases, the patients are gradually rejuvenated without sequelae.
However, such as SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also associated with high morbidity and mortality for the most serious cases.
As a result, a prognostic model is essential for health agencies to be able to prioritize their services, especially in areas with limited resources.
Based on the clinical studies carried out so far, the prognosibilities that may or may be associated with the prognosis of patients with COVID-19 (Table (Table 33):
Age: age was the main factor in SARS prognosis, and this also seems to be the case for COVID-19.
COVID-19 mainly affects individuals aged between 30 and 65, with 47.7% over the age of 50, according to an 8,866 case study, as described above.
Extensive care patients were more likely to experience underlying comorbidities and complications, and were markedly older (median age 66 years versus 51 years), thus suggesting age as a prognostic factor for patients suffering from COVID-19.
Sex: SARS-CoV-2 infected more men than women (0.3/1010,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as described above.
Comorbidities and Complications: COVID-19 patients requiring intensive care are more prone to acute heart lesions and arrhythmia.
Heart events were also the leading cause of death in SARS patients.
It has been established that SARS-CoV-2 can also bind to ACE2-positive cholangiocytes, which can lead to liver dysfunction in patients with COVID-19.
It should be noted that the age and presence of underlying health problems are strongly correlated and can interfere with each other.
Absolute laboratory results: the level of C-reactive protein (CRP) in the blood reflects the severity of inflammation or tissue lesions; it has been advanced as a potential pro prognostic factor for the disease, response to treatment and recovery.
Correlation was also proposed between CRP levels and severity and prognosis for COVID-19 was also proposed.
In addition, an elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT) and creatine kinase (CK) may also contribute to prognosis.
These enzymes are widely expressed within several organs, including the heart and the liver, and are released from tissue lesions.
They therefore traditionally constitute markers of cardiac or hepatic dysfunction.
Major Clinical Symptoms: Chest X-ray results and the temporal progression of clinical symptoms should be examined in conjunction with other elements for the determination of pro prognosis and complications of COVID-19.
Using steroids: As described above, steroids are immunosuppressants commonly used for adjunctive therapy for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory lesions.
Since high-dose corticosteroid intake has been widely used for severe cases of SARS, many survivors experienced avascular osteonecrosis resulting in permanent incapacity and poor quality of life.
Thus, if necessary, the administration of steroids to patients suffering from COVID-19 should be limited to a low and short duration.
Mental Stress: As described above, the COVID-19 outbreak has led to a large number of cases of exceptional stress, with patients often experiencing long quarantine periods, extreme uncertainty as well as the death of relatives or other patients.
It is imperative to offer long-term psychological support and support in order to help these individuals overcome this stress and return to normal life.
Degree studies carried out so far, COVID-19 appears to have different epidemiological characteristics from SARS.
In addition to replicating in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 also responds effectively into the upper respiratory tract and results in little or no symptoms in the first phase of the infection, such as the coronavirus that causes common colds.
As a result, individuals that have recently been infected or incubated can produce a large amount of viruses during their daily activities, significantly hampering the control of the epidemic.
However, it was considered that SARS-CoV was transmitted by seriously ill patients, with a very low probability of transmission in the first phase.
The current COVID-19 epidemic is therefore much more serious and difficult to control than SARS.
Hundred efforts are currently being made in China, including the containment of Wuhan and neighbouring cities, as well as the continued quarantine of almost the entire population with a view to interrupt the transmission of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these measures have had a dramatic impact on the economy as well as in other sectors of the country, the number of new cases is declining, indicating a slowdown in the epidemic.
The most optimistic estimates suggest that the epidemic will end in March and that the decay phase will take 3 to 4 months.
However, not all the experts share this optimism.
Paul Hunter, et al., estimates that COVID-19, which appears to be significantly more infectious than SARS, will not extinct in 2020.
Ira Longini, et al., established a model to predict the outcome of the epidemic and concluded that SARS-CoV-2 could infect two-thirds of the world’s population.
A Canadian group reported that SARS-CoV-2 had been detected in the samples from the mean nasal horn and throat of patients recovered, 2 weeks after their discharge from the hospital, indicating that the newly identified virus could become a cyclic episode such as influenza.
However, encouraging signs were observed in China with the gradual decrease in new cases, suggesting that the strategies implemented would have worked.
According to initial predictions, the Ebola virus involved up to 1 million cases and half a million deaths.
However, thanks to strict quarantine and isolation, the disease was finally placed under control.
It is possible, as for SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2 weakens in terms of infectiveness and eventually turns extinct or evolves into a less pathogen-contaminating virus co-existing with humans.
Comparisation of the COVID-19 outbreak with SARS and MERS (FIG. (Fig.
SARS-CoV-2 is highly transmissible by cough or sneezing, and also possibly through direct-contact with contaminated materials.
The virus has also been identified in stools, thus revealing a new possibility of feco-oral transmission.
A recent study of 138 patients revealed that 41% of the cases were possibly due to nosocomial infections, involving 17 patients with previous diseases and 40 healthcare professionals.
Precautionary measures should thus be taken to protect individuals, including healthcare professionals, social workers, relatives and coarseae of patients, and even anyone who may come into contact with patients or those infected.
The first possible line of defence to reduce the risk of infection is the wearing of facial masks; the use of N95 (1860) surgical dust and ventilatory masks helps to control the spread of the viruses.
Surgical dust masks prevent the projection of potentially infected droplets or their deposition on surfaces, where they could be transmitted to other people.
However, only masks N95 (1860) provide protection against the inhalation of virions from 10 to 80 nm, with only 5 per virions capable of fully penetrating; SARS-CoV-2 is similar to SARS-CoV in pruning, since both measure about 85 nm.
Since the particles manage to pass through five surgical masks stacked on top of each other, it is imperative that healthcare professionals in direct contact with patients wore N95 masks (1860s) and not surgical dusk.
In addition to masks, healthcare professionals should wear adjusted insulation blouses in order to further reduce contact with the viruses.
Viruses can also infect an individual via the eye.
On January 22, 2020, a doctor was diagnosed with SARS-CoV-2 even though it wore an N95-mask; the virus may have penetrating its organevice through the eye due to ignition.
As a result, healthcare professionals should also wear transparent visors or protective goggles in contact with patients.
It is strongly recommended for populations in affected or potentially affected areas, it is strongly recommended to wash your hands with disinfectant soap more often than usual, to remain confined as possible and to limit any contact with potentially infected persons.
It is advisable to maintain a distance of one metre with patients.
These measures represent effective ways to reduce the risk of infection and prevent the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 is a novel virus for humans, its high degree of homology with SARS-CoV, as reported on 7 January 2020, must have put China on high alert following the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, on January 19, 2020, the director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Control and Prevention reassured the population by saying that the new virus was uncontagious, that it had limited-negligibility and that it would not be difficult to prevent and contain the disease.
This message led to a noticeable relaxation, especially at a time when the entire country was preparing for the Spring Festival, and the critical opportunity to contain the disease in Wuhan was missed.
China’s disease control agencies must draw lessons and take the necessary action.
For for example, these agencies must (1) be more cautious in their public announcements, as each word counts and can affect the behaviour and decisions of the population; (2) show more sensitivity and reactivity to the unusual data of clinics rather than waiting for formal reports by doctors or official bodies; (3) being more restrictive in order to contain a potential epidemic at its beginning rather than trying to re-ensure the population; and (4) carry out more
The COVID-19 epidemic caused by the new SARS-CoV-2 virus began at the end of December 2019.
In less than two months, at the time of the report’s writing, it had spread throughout China as well as to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
Given that this virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms of COVID-19 are also similar to those of SAR SARS, the COVID-19 epidemic gives rise as an impression of déjà vu.
However, there are significant differences between COVID-19 and SARS, taking into account with a view to containing the epidemic and treating patients.
COVID-19 affects older people more than young people and more men than women; the severity and mortality rate are also higher among older people.
SARS has a higher mortality than COVID-19 (10.91% compared to 1.44%).
Transmission of COVID-19 virus is possible even in the absence of Symptoms, while SAR SARS was mostly transmitted by severely ill patients, making it much more difficult to contain the spread of COVID-19.
This partly explains why SARS-CoV-2 spread much faster and much more widely than SARS-CoV.
Correct testing for SARS-CoV-2 RNA may be negative for some patients with COVID-19.
On the other hand, recovering patients may again be resistant to the virus.
This considerably increases the risk of propagation.
Despite rapid progress in COVID-19 research, a number of critical issues remain unresolved, including:
Where does SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although 96% genetic homology has been established between SARS-CoV-2 and two SARS coronaviruses from bats, this is not enough to conclusively conclude that SARS-CoV-2 comes from chiroptera.
What is the intermediate species that has made it possible to transmit the virus from the initial host, e.g. bats, to humans?
Unanswered questions 1 and 2, it is impossible to interrupt transmission effectively, and the epidemic can recover at any time.
Molecular modelling and biochemical analyses have shown that SARS-CoV-2 binds to ACE2, but how exactly does the virus enter airway cells and how does it cause pathological changes?
Is the virus also binding to ACE2-examining ACE2 cells?
Without clear answers to these questions, it is impossible to define a rapid and accurate diagnosis or an effective treatment.
How long will the epidemic last?
How does the virus evolve genetically during human-to-human transmission?
Were it going to evolve into a global pandemic, disappear like SARS or resurfacing regularly like the flu?
It is essential, even if it may take time, to get answers to all of these questions as well as to many others.
However, no matter how much money this is, we have no choice but to put an end to this epidemic as soon as possible in order to resume the normal course of our existence.
Usually the incubation period of these two viruses is less than one week, followed by approximately 2 weeks of illness.
Only rare immunocompromised patients experienced severe infection of the lower respiratory tract.
The first case of SARS dates back at the end of 2002 in Guangdong Province, China.
Excluding super-transmitters, it was estimated that each case could lead to approximately two secondary cases with an incubation period of between 4 and 7 days, the peak of the viral load appearing on the 10th day of the disease.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially experience myalgia, headache, fever, malaise and chills, followed by dyspnoea, cweath and respiratory distress as late symptoms.
Lympgaspane, distorted liver function tests and high creatine kinase are common abnormalities observed in laboratory testing for SARS suspision.
Diffuse alveolar lesions, epithelial cell proliferation and an increase in macrophages are also observed in patients with SARS.
Approximately 20 to 30 per cent of patients then require intensive care and mechanical dissolvency.
In addition to the lower respiratory tract, several organs including the gastrointestinal tract, the liver and the kidney can also be infected in these severe cases, often accompanied by cytokine shock, which can be fatal especially in immunocompromised patients.
Since then, enormous efforts have been devoted to HCoV research.
HCoV-NL63 was isolated in a 7-month child in the Netherlands at the end of 2004.
Originally it was prevalent in young children, the elderly and immunocompromised patients suffering from respiratory diseases.
The presence of coryza, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchiolitis is common in the disease caused by HCoV-NL63.
Another self-sustained study has described the lounging of the same virus from the nasal sampling of an 8-month-old boy with pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although it has been identified in the Netherlands, it is in fact present all over the world.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also called croup.
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 was lounged in a 71-year-old man hospitalized due to pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
Like HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 occurs globally, causing moderate respiratory diseases.
Typically, when these HCoVs acquire the ability to transmit themselves effectively and to sustain themselves continuously in humans, they also become less virulent or pathogenic.
Another secondary epidemic occurred in South Korea in 2015, with 186 confirmed cases.
The clinical manifestations of MERS resembling those of SARS and are characterised by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many MERS patients have also developed acute renal failure, which is characteristic of MERS compared to HCoV conditions.
More than 30 per cent of patients experience gastrointestinal symptoms such as diarrhoeonation and vomiting.
As of February 14, 2020, more than 2,500 laboratory-confirmed cases were reported with a high death rate of 34.4%, making MERS-CoV one of the deadliest viruses known in humans.
Diarrhoea is also observed in some patients.
SARS-CoV-2 was apparently less pathogenic, but more transmissible than SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Asymptomatic subjects infected with SARS-CoV-2 have been reported and could contribute to its rapid spread around the world.
Comparrating and opposing SARS-CoV-2 with the other six HCoV revealed very interesting similarities and differences.
Firstly, the incubation period and the duration of HCoV diseases are very similar.
In this regard, SARS-CoV-2 follows the general trend of the other six HCoVs.
Secondly, the severity of COVID-19 symptoms lies between SARS-CoV and the four Community HCoV (i.e. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
On the other hand, a small sub-group of severe cases of COVID-19 is also observed as in the case of SARS-CoV infection, although the proportion is slightly lower.
Finally, like the other HCoV, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in stool samples.
However, the characteristics of SARS-CoV-2, in particular its transmissibility, pathogenicity and long-sustaining post-transmovement to humans will have an influence on the ultimate fate of the current COVID-19 outbreak.
The four Community HCoVs causing moderate symptoms adapted well to humans.
In other words, both could be the survivor of previous HCoV pandemics.
In order for this to happen, HCoVs must retaliate in humans in a sufficient proportion to allow the accumulation of adaptive mutations that offset host-restricting factors.
From this point of view, the more the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic persists, the greater the number of people infected, the more likely that the virus fully adapts to humans.
Adheres well, its transmission between men will be difficult to stop by quarantine or other infection control measures.
For many for many years, the four CoVs acquired in the community circulate within the population, triggering a simple cold in immunocompetent subjects.
These viruses don't require an animal reservoir.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which are highly pathogenic, did not adapt well to humans and their transmission between men is not sustainable.
They need to maintain and propagate in their zoonotic reservoirs and seek an expedition opportunity to reach potential human targets, probably via one or more intermediate hosts and amplifiers.
SARS-CoV-2 has characteristics similar to SARS-CoV/MERS-CoVs and four community-acquired HCoV.
However, it is more pathogenic than community-grinding HCoV and less pathogenic than SARS-Cov or MERS-CoV.
It has yet to be seen whether it will fully adapt to human beings and will circulate among human beings without a reservoir or an intermediate pet host.
Before discussing the animal origins of HCoVs, it will be very useful for us to discuss the definitions and characteristics of scalable, natural hoses, reservoirs, intermediates and amplifiers of HCoV.
An animal acts as an evolutionary host of an HCoV if it houses a near ascendant sharing high homology of the nucleotide sequence.
The original virus has often adapted well and is non-mpathogenic to its host.
In the same way, a reservoir host houses HCoV on a continuous and long-term basis.
On the other hand, HCoVs can also adapt to the intermediate host and even establish long-term enmdemicity.
In this case, the intermediate host becomes a natural reservoir host.
In particular, since 80 per cent of Guangzhou animals on the Guangzhou market have anti-SASA-CoV antibodies, the chances that several small mammal mammal species may also serve as amplifier intermediate hosts cannot be excluded.
These bush-boats are pro-positive to anti-SARS-CoV antibodies and to the genomic sequence of SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3.
These studies laid the foundation for a new concept, according to which bats are hosts of emerging human pathogens.
An angiotensinc converting enzyme human 2 (ACE2) is not known as SARS-CoV receptor.
Despite high homology between these two viruses, it is generally believed that WIV1 is not the immediate relative of SARS-CoV and that bats are not the immediate reservoir host of SARS-CoV.
This is why bats cannot be the intermediate reservoir host of the MERS-CoV.
In addition, studies conducted in the Middle East have demonstrated that comedones are seropositive to specific MERS-CoV neutralising antibodies, just like Middle-Pain camels present in several African countries.
In addition, invented camels excrete the virus not only via the respiratory tract, but also facial, which is also the main route of excretion of bats.
We cannot rule out the possibility that pangolin is one of the intermediate animal hosts of SARS-CoV-2.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is even less important than that which separates beta-CoVs associated with SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 of pangolin.
Recombination is a widespread factor in the evolution of beta-CoV.
Scissors remain divided over the immediate zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2.
In addition to highly Pathically Paculated HCoV, the zoonotic origins of HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-HKU1 have also been studied.
Phylogenetic evidence indicates that HCoV-NL63 and HCoV-2229E may both be derived from bat CoV, while parent HCoV-OC43 and HCoV-HKU1 were found in rodents.
On the other hand, HCoV-229E was genetically associated with another bat CoV, called Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008, detected in Ghana, while camelides were also suspected to be intermediate hosts.
When HCoV-OC43 surpassed species barrier to infect humans from domestic cattle around 1890, a respiratory pandemic was reported.
The history of HCoV-229E inter-species transmission is less clear.
Alpha-CoV of the bat close to HCoV-229E were found.
First of all, unlike alpacas, humans can have contact with bats in a shared ecological niche.
On the other hand, humans have close contact with alpacas.
Second, bat alpha-CoV related to HCoV-229E are numerous and non-pathogenic in bats, while alpaca alpha-CoV caused an epidemic of respiratory disease in infected animals.
Finally, alpaca alpha-CoV has not been found in wild animals.
In fact, bats are a direct source of human pathogenic viruses, including rabies virus, Ebola, Nipah Virus and Hendra Virus.
It is therefore not astonishing that bats can pass the HCoV-229E directly to humans.
Another possibility, while bat alpha-CoV serve as a genetic reservoir of HCoV-229E, alpacas and comedariats could serve as intermediate hosts transmitting viruses to humans, exactly as in the case of MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is an excellent example of inter-species transmission from dromed-bearing bats and comedrobes to humans.
Evolving MERS-CoV origin found in bats is known due to its initial identification and has also been reinforced by subsequent discoveries.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has been present in comedrobes for decades.
It adapted well in these camels, which have moved from mid-host to stable natural reservoir hosts.
Contrary to the role of comedians in MERS-CoV transmission, the pangolin role in SARS-CoV-2 transmission, if any, is different.
In particular, pangolin beta-CoV is highly pathogenic in pangolins.
A number of inter-species transmission of SARS-CoV-2 from animals to humans were included or excluded from future studies.
Integrally, mouth bats could be the reservoir host of a SARS-CoV-2-related virus almost identical to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans could share an ecological bloom with bats through decaying or charcoal mines.
Secondly, pangolins could be one of the intermediate amplifier hosts to which a SARS-CoV-2 virus has recently been introduced.
Humans contract the virus by dissecting and embroidering of game meat.
Many mammals, including domestic animals, may be sensitive to SARS-CoV-2.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, the recombination and adaptation of SARS-CoV-2 may have taken place within a third species having been in contact with both bats and pangolins.
Research on the animal origins of SARS-CoV-2 is ongoing.
Comparrated to other single-strand-strengthened RNA viruses, the estimated COV mutation rates could be considered “moderate” to “high” with an average substitution rate of 10-4 substitutions per year per site, depending on the adaptation phase of CoV to new hosts.
Nevertheless, CoV mutation rates are about one million times higher than their hosts.
In addition, mutation rates are often high when Co-CVs have not adapted well to the host.
Compared to SARS-CoV, having a high mutation rate, the SARS-CoV-2 mutate rate is apparently lower, suggesting a higher rate of human adaptation.
Presume that it has already adapted to another host close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to MERS-CoV, which is well suited to comeomies.
In theory, genetic drifting is unlikely to render vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2 ineffective in the short term.
Thirdly, Co-Vs randomly and frequently change models when replicating the NRA through a single “choice-copy” mechanism.
Phylogenetic evidence of natural recombination has been found for both HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV- OC43, as well as CoV seen in animals, such as SL-CoV and batCoV-HKU9.
Virus-host interaction related to transmission
In addition to these three viral factors indicated above, the viral interaction with a host-receptor is another key factor influencing interspecies transmission.
The recombination of SARS-CoV is taken as a typical example here; it also shows evidence of positive selection during inter-species transmission episodes.
In other words, these two amino acid substitutions can be a key factor in adapting the virus to humans.
It is interesting to note that SARS-CoV-2 shares the same cellular receiver as SARS-CoV.
A difference of 30 per cent between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in unit S1 means that the affinity for binding this S-protein with human ACE2 may have been impaired.
Indeed, an electron cryomicroscopy study indicates an affinity 10- to 20 times higher than that observed between human ACE2 and the SARS-CoV S protein.
It will also be interesting to determine whether another co-receiver can be required for the transmission of SARS-CoV-2.
Strangely, HCoV-NL63 also bind to ACE2 but with a different part of the S protein.
There are many other HCoV-receives, such as aminopeptidase N for HCoV-229E, and 9-O-acetylated sialic acid for HCoV-OC43.
The divergence of host proteins between humans and natural reservoir hosts of HCoVs, such as bats, comedrobes and rodents, could constitute a barrier to inter-species transmission.
Emergence of new HCoV: back to square
The diversity of bat CoVs offers many opportunities for the emergence of new HCoVs.
In this sense, bat CoV are genetic reserves of HCoV.
Among SARS-CoV accessory proteins, ORF8 was identified as important in adapting to humans, as SARS-CoV-related bat viruses were isolated, but encoding diverging ORF8 proteins.
This elimination splits ORF8 into ORF8a and ORF8b and is anticipated as an adaptive mutation that promotes host change.
Recombination sites have also been identified in nsp9, in most nsp10 and in some nsp14.
Similarly, MERS-CoV epidemic has been shown to experience recombination episodes between different lines, which have occurred in comedariains in Saudi Arabia.
While an ORF4 could be observed in bat and camel viruses associated with HCoV-229E, alpaca alpha-CoV has simple nucleotide insertion, resulting in lagging of the reading.
Finally, the development of the new HCoV is also stimulated by the selective pressure exerted in their reservoir hosts.
Asymptomatic cases or moderate symptoms have been detected when bats were infected with CoV, indicating a mutual adaptation between Coves and bats.
It seemed that the bats had adapted well to CoV from an anatomical and physiological point of view.
For example, abnormalities in the activation of pro-inflammatorium response in bats effectively reduce the pathology unleashed by Co-V.
In addition, the high level of reactive Oxynoxy Derivatives (ORDs) generated by the high metabolic activity of bats could both suppress CoV replication and affect "releeping" by exoribonuclease, which would create selective pressure and give rise to highly pathogenic viral strains when introduced into a new host.
More pathogenic strains of COV could also evolve by recombination, leading to the acquisition of new proteins or protein characteristics for host adaptation.
Thus, it is no coincidence that three new HCoVs have appeared over the past two decades.
