Co-LoVs have come back in the spotlight due to the recent SARS-CoV-2 outbreak.
The study of COVs in bats and other animals greatly altered our perception of the role of zoonotic origins and animal reservoirs in transmitting HCoV to humans.
Numerous factual facts have shown that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 come from the bat and are transmitted to humans by intermediate hosts.
Given the fact that SARS-CoV infection originates from contact between humans and civets present on the markets, the closure of the markets for fresh products and the octopate slaughter there could have put an effective end to the SARS outbreak.
According to the same reasoning, pangolins should be withdrawn from the markets for fresh produce to avoid the transmission of zoonoses, taking into account the discovery of numerous pangolin beta-CoV lines closely related to SARS-CoV-2
However, the question of whether SARS-CoV-2 is transmitted to humans by pangolins and other mammal mammals and, if any, how this transmission occurs, will remain to be determined in subsequent studies.
