In 11.8% of the deaths reported by the Chinese National Health Commission, heart lesions due to high troponin levels or cardiac arrest were observed.
According to March data from the United States, 89 per cent of persons in hospital had pre-existing conditions; the availability of medical resources and the socio-economic conditions of a region can also have an impact on mortality.
Estimates of the disease mortality rate vary because of these regional differences, but also due to methodological difficulties.
Underreporting of moderate cases may lead to an overvaluation of mortality rates.
However, the fact that deaths are the result of previously contracted cases may indicate that the current mortality rate is underestimated.
Smokers were 1.4-fold more prone to developing severe symptoms of COVID-19 and are 2.4 times more likely to go into intensive care or die compared to non-smokers.
The Hong Kong hospital administration observed a 20 to 30 per cent drop in the respiratory capacity in some people cured of the disease, and pulmonary psychitassies suggest organ lesions.
This can also lead to post-intensive-care post-healing syndrome.
In March 2020, there was no doubt whether a previous infection gave effective long-term immunity in those cured of the disease.
Immunity is considered likely, based on the behaviour of the other coronaviruses, but cases of COVID-19 cured people followed by positive coronavirus tests at a later date have been reported.
These cases are seen as a worsening of latent infection rather than re-infection.
It is believed that the virus is natural and of animal origin, through the spread of the infection.
The real origin of the virus is unknown, but in December 2019 the spread of the infection was carried out almost solely by human-to-human transmission.
A study of the first 41 confirmed cases of COVID-19 published in January 2020 in The Lancet revealed that the oldest occurrence of symptoms dates back to December 1, 2019.
Authental WHO publications indicated the first onset of symptoms on 8 December 2019.
Several measures are generally used to assess mortality.
These figures vary by region and over time, and are influenced by the volume of tests, the quality of the health-care system, therapeutic options, time since the beginning of the epidemic, as well as demographic characteristics such as age, gender and health status.
At the end of 2019, the SMTP affected CIM-10 U07.1 Emergency Pathological Codes for Laboratory-Confirmed Deaths of SARS-CoV-2 Infection and U07.2 for Clinical or Epidemiologically Diagnosed Clinically or Epidemiologically Uncontailed SARS-CoV-2 Introductory Infection.
According to statistics from Johns-Hopkins University, the worldwide death/case ratio is 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191) as of April 17, 2020.
Other measures include lethality rate (FRC) which reflects the proportion of diagnosed individuals who die from the disease and the rate of infectious lethality (IRF) that reflects the percentage of people infected (diagnosed or undiagnosed) from a disease.
These stats are not limited over time and follow a specific population of contamination at the end of the disease.
Although not all those infected develop antibodies, the presence of antibodies may indicate the number of people who have been infected.
In the epicentre of the epidemic in Italy in Castiglione d’Adda, a small village with 4,600 inhabitants, 80 (1.7%) have already died.
In Gangelt, the disease has spread through festivals, and to young people, resulting in a relatively low mortality rate, and all COVID-19-related deaths may not have been officially categorized.
Moreover, the German health-care system has not been overwhelmed.
In the Netherlands, about 3 per cent of people may have antibodies, according to an assessment by blood donors.
69 deaths (0.004% of the population) were Confirmed Related to COVID-19.
The impact of the Pandemic and the Mortality Rate are different among men and women.
Mortgage is higher among humans in studies in China and Italy.
The risk is the highest among 50-year-old men, the difference between men and women disappearing only at the age of 90.
In China, the death rate was 2.8 per cent among men and 1.7 per cent for women.
Precise reasons for this difference between the sexes are unknown, but genetics and behavioral factors could be included.
Immunological differences according to gender, the lower prevalence of smoking in women and the development in men of comorbidities such as hypertension at an younger age than in women may have contributed to a higher mortality in men.
In Europe, 57% of infected individuals were men and 72% of people who died from COVID-19 were men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government did not collect data associated with the COVID-19 type.
Studies have shown that viral diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS affect men and women differently.
A higher percentage of health-skills, in particular Nurses, are women and are at an increased risk of being exposed to the virus.
The World Health Organisation announced on February 11, 2020 that the official name of the disease would be “ COVID-19”.
WHO director-general Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus explained that CO means corona, VI means virus and D means disease, 19 being the first identification of the epidemic: December 31, 2019.
This name was chosen to avoid references to a specific geographical location (e.g. China), an animal species or a group of people, in accordance with international nomenclature recommendations aimed at preventing stigmatization.
In addition, the WHO is using “D COVID-19 Virus” and “Vext Responsible for COVID-19” in its public communication.
The disease and the virus are both commonly called “rapegraviruses”.
During the initial epidemic in Wuhan, China, the virus and the disease were often called “corroavirus” and “Wuhan Cor Coronaeration.”
In January 2020, the OMS recommended the use of provisional disease names 2019-nCov and Acute Respiratory Disease 2019-nCoV for Virus and Disease, in accordance with the 2015 Guidelines against the Use of Sites in Disease and Viral Names.
The official names COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were published on 11 February 2020.
Taking into account the capacity limits of standard supply chains, some digital manufacturers print care equipment such as swabs and respirator parts.
For example, when an Italian hospital needed an emergency respirator valve and the supplier could not supply it on time, a local start-up reconcluded and printed the required 100 valves over one night.
Following the initial COVID-19 epidemic, conspiracy theories, misinformation and misinformation emerged about the origin, scope, prevention, treatment and other aspects of the disease and quickly spread online.
It seems that humans are able to propagate the disease to certain other animals.
The studies failed to find evidence of viral replication in pigs, ducks and chickens.
No medicines or vaccines are approved to treat the disease.
International research on COVID-19 vaccines and drugs is currently carried out by government organisations, academic groups and industrial researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization launched the SOLIDARITY trial to assess the therapeutic effects of four existing antiviral compounds with the most promising effectiveness.
There is no vaccine available, but several agencies are actively developing vaccine candidates.
Prior work on SARS-CoV is being used because SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 both use ACE 2 receptor to enter human cells.
Three vaccination strategies are being researched.
First of all, the researchers are trying to create an entire virus vaccine.
The use of such a virus, whether inactive or dancing, aims to cause a rapid immune response of the human body in the event of further COVID-19 infection.
A second strategy, sub-united vaccines, aims to create a vaccine that sensitizes the immune system to certain sub-units of the virus.
In SARS-CoV-2, this research focuses on the spicule S protein that helps the virus infiltrate the ACE 2 enzyme receptor.
A third strategy is vaccines based on nu nucleic acid (vaccination with DNA or an RNA, a new technique for the creation of vaccination).
Experimental vaccines derived from all these strategies should be tested in terms of safety and efficacy. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of a vaccine started with four volunteers in Seattle.
Antiboggan-independent activation has been suggested as a potential challenge in the development of a SARS-CoV-2 vaccine, but it is controversial.
As of April 2020, more than 300 active clinical trials are ongoing.
Seven trials evaluated previously approved treatments against malaria, including four studies involving hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Reconverted antiviral drugs constitute the majority of Chinese research, with nine phase III clinical trials of remdesivir in several countries whose reports are due at the end of April.
Dynamic clinical development analysis of COVID-19 vaccine candidates was also in place as of April 2020, Several existing antiviral drugs are being evaluated for the treatment of COVID-19, including remdesivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, lopaour/ritonavir and lopinavir/ritonavir in combination with interferon beta.
Textile data suggest the effectiveness of remdesivir in March 2020.
Clinical improvements have been observed in patients treated with remdesivir for compassionate use.
Phase III clinical trials are underway in the United States, China, and Italy; chloroquine, previously used to treat malaria, was studied in China in February 2020, with preliminary results.
However, some require an independent analysis of the research.
The Chinese and Korean health authorities recommend the use of chloroquine.
However, virology in Wuhan, although recommending a daily dose of one gram, indicates that twice this dose is highly dangerous and could be lethal.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA authorized the use of hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine under an authorisation for emergency use at the discretion of doctors caring for COVID-19 patients.
Preliminary data indicate that high doses of ribavirin are needed to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
Nitazoxanide has been recommended for the continuation of an in-Vision Study after Demonstration of Low SSAR-CoV-2 Concentration. Studies have been shown that the initial spicule protein initiating using the serine 2 transmembrane protease (TMPRSS2) is essential for the entry of SARS-CoV-2 via interaction with ACE 2 receptor.
Choliquine and hydroxychloroquine studies with or without azzithromycin have major limitations that have prevented the medical community from adopting these treatments without further studies.oseltamivir does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro and has no known role in the treatment of COVID-19.
Cytokinetic shock can be a complication at subsequent stages of severe COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxyxychloroquine may have cytokinetic anti-shock properties. Tocilizumab was included in the treatment guidelines by the Chinese National Health Commission after a small study has been carried out.
It is in the non-randomised phase 2 test in Italy after showing positive results in people with a severe form of the disease.
Associated with a serum ferritin blood test to identify cytokinetic shock, it should counteract such changes that are suspected to be responsible for the death of certain affected people.
Interleukin-6-receptor antagonist was approved by the FDA based on retrospective case studies for the treatment of steroid-resiscul-refractory cytokine release syndrome induced by a different Causibilated Cause, treatment with CAR T cells in 2017.
To date, there is no randomised controlled evidence that tocilizumab is an effective SCR treatment.
The transfer of concentrated and purified antibodies produced by the immune systems of people curing COVID-19 to people in need is being studied as a non-vaccine method of passive immunization.
This strategy was tested for SARS with few conclusive results.
Viral Neutral Neutralisation is the expected mode of action whereby passive antibody-based treatment can generate defence against SARS-CoV-2.
However, other mechanisms such as antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity and/or phagocytosis may be possible.
Other forms of passive anti-antibiotic treatment, e.g. using manufactured monoclonal antibodies, are under development.
Serum production by convalescent people, consisting of the liquid part of the blood of cured patients and containing specific antibodies against this virus, could be increased for more rapid deployment.
